# Notice: where this export's files come from ({{TOOLS_VERSION}}, experimental)

This is a factual statement, not a licence grant.

## Origin

Every file in this export comes from the **Avrana Party repository** (private) and was copied by a
script, or generated by that script from constants in it; `MANIFEST.json` names the kind of each file and
the commit it was built from. The copied Python files have **unchanged code**: comments and docstrings that
named internal material were removed from them, `MANIFEST.json` flags which files were touched, and the
build checks that the code is identical once docstrings are stripped. The `avrana/party/` modules, the
`avrana/contracts/party_games.py` module and the `avrana/__init__.py` package root are small generated
stand-ins, and `contracts/capabilities.v0.json` is a reduced copy with the real capability names and
placeholder probes, labels and summaries. `README.md` is written for this export and
`AVRGAME-PACKAGE-FORMAT.md` is an excerpt of a design note of that repository.

## Redistribution terms: an open owner decision

The Avrana Party repository does not state a licence: its package metadata names one, but the
repository carries no licence file, and its owner has not decided the terms under which these files may
be redistributed or reused outside the project. That is an **open owner decision**; nothing in this
export decides it, and nothing here grants any right. Ask the owner before passing this export on or
building on it.

## Things this export reveals about the platform

Besides what any reader of the README learns (the package format, its limits, the conditions a game runs
under on an appliance, the protocol version strings and the fact that a Party exists), a build-time check
looked for names of internal material (tracker ids, decision numbers, other games, hardware, paths,
addresses, people) and found only these places where the tool itself needs them:

{{REVEALED}}

## Decision left to the owner: `extract`

The command `extract` is the only one that writes files for a package it did not make. It is kept in the
export because the Python file that defines the command line is the project's own, unchanged. Its refusals
(unsafe names, duplicate and case-colliding names, device names, encrypted entries, oversized and
decompression-bomb archives, a destination that is not new or empty, a link in the destination) are tested
against the same archives as the project's own tool. The owner may prefer an export without it.

## How it is meant to be used

The export is produced for the repository's owner to hand to game developers at the owner's
discretion. The command that builds it only writes a local file; it publishes nothing and contacts
nothing, and the project's CI does not keep it as a download. If you received it, you received it from
the owner or someone the owner told; do not assume it is public.
